> before weighting which inconveience is less

I do not necessarily agree with this: a "better heuristic" is
essentially a hack built on an existing hack - sometimes "less is
more", and the simple, clean solution (and which is also "correct",
since terminal cleanup is shell's responsibility and NOT FFmpeg's; try
e.g cat /bin/ls) is the current patch.

>
> for example, you say that a 2 line fix is possible for the shell
> config.
> Can this fix be detected easily ?

At least in zsh, ttyctl gives a string that can be used to check this.
AFAIK, there is no such method in bash. However, please note that
doing this at runtime is silly (and perhaps nontrivial). It will need
to go in configure; and a user may very well change his or her shell
settings, build again (without rerunning configure), and make this
useless. This only reinforces the fact that this is a heuristic.

> if so the heuristic could be
> limited to the case that does not have this fix
> it would be strictly better than the current code in git, leaving
> the heuristic only for users who have not added these 2 lines

If better=correct behavior in more circumstances, then yes; if
better=cleaner or simpler code; then no.

